`Routes()` / `chi.Walk()` silently drop a handler registered on the same pattern as a `Route()` / `Mount()`

When a router has both a real handler on a path and a sub-router mounted at that same path, the real handler disappears from route introspection. Requests to it are served correctly, but `chi.Walk()` and `Routes()` don't report it, so our generated route docs and permission checks miss the endpoint.

Minimal reproduction (chi v5, current master):

```go
r := chi.NewRouter()
h := func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {}

r.Route("/foo", func(r chi.Router) {
    r.Route("/bar", func(r chi.Router) {
        r.Get("/{id}", h)
    })
    r.Get("/bar", h)
})

chi.Walk(r, func(method, route string, _ http.Handler, _ ...func(http.Handler) http.Handler) error {
    fmt.Println(method, route)
    return nil
})
```

Actual output: only `GET /foo/bar/{id}`.
Expected output: both `GET /foo/bar` and `GET /foo/bar/{id}`.

The same thing is visible with `Routes()` directly: after `r.Route("/bar", ...)` plus `r.Get("/bar", h)` on the same router, there is no `/bar` entry at all. Expected: a `/bar` entry whose `Handlers` contains exactly the `GET` handler and whose `SubRoutes` is nil (the internal placeholder the mount uses must not show up as an extra handler, e.g. under `"*"`). A plain `r.Route("/api", ...)` with nothing else on `/api` should keep reporting a single `/api/*` entry with its `SubRoutes`, as it does today, and middleware propagation through `Walk()` must not change.
